Data Analysis & Insights Report
Task 2 – Data Analysis and Insights Generation using Python

Overview
The objective of this analysis was to explore and generate actionable insights from the
automotive repair dataset using Python. The focus was to clean the data, identify key patterns
in repairs, analyze cost and usage behavior, and extract tags from customer feedback to
support data-driven decision-making.

Column-Wise Analysis
The dataset contained 100 records and 52 columns including vehicle details, repair
descriptions, costs, and feedback.
Data types: Numeric, categorical, datetime, and text.
Nulls: One column (CAMPAIGN_NBR) was fully missing and removed.
Key columns: CUSTOMER_VERBATIM, CORRECTION_VERBATIM, TOTALCOST, KM, and
CAUSAL_PART_NM held strong analytical value.

Data Cleaning
Standardized column names (uppercase, underscores).
Filled missing categorical values with mode and numeric values with median.
Fixed text inconsistencies (case, spacing) and capped numeric outliers using the IQR
method.
Final dataset had no missing or duplicate records and consistent formats across all
columns.

Critical Columns Identified
1. CUSTOMER_VERBATIM – Real customer complaints.
2. CORRECTION_VERBATIM – Technician’s actions.
3. CAUSAL_PART_NM – Faulty part causing issue.
4. TOTALCOST – Repair cost impact.
5. KM – Vehicle usage, indicating wear and reliability.

These fields link customer experience, technical repair data, and financial implications, giving
the clearest insights for stakeholders.

Visual Insights
Top Causal Parts: Steering-related components appeared most frequently, suggesting
recurring issues.
Repair Cost Distribution: Right-skewed pattern showed most repairs were low-cost,
with few expensive outliers.
KM vs. Total Cost: Scatter analysis showed a positive correlation — higher mileage led
to higher repair costs.
These insights highlight both cost concentration areas and parts with high failure frequency.
Generated Tags from Free Text
Using TF-IDF, tags were extracted from combined customer and technician comments.
Common tags like “steering,” “module,” “wheel,” and “replacement” summarized feedback
themes and recurring failures. This approach can automate complaint grouping and speed up
root-cause analysis.

Key Insights & Recommendations
Frequent Issues: Steering and module-related repairs dominate.
Cost Trend: Higher vehicle KM relates to higher repair costs.
Improvement Areas: Prioritize top recurring parts for quality checks.
Automation: Use text tags to auto-classify complaints and detect patterns early.

Actionable Steps:
1. Focus on high-cost, frequent-failure parts.
2. Monitor cost trends vs. KM to plan preventive maintenance.
3. Integrate tag analytics in dashboards for faster fault detection.

Conclusion
The analysis successfully cleaned, explored, and summarized the dataset to reveal major cost
drivers and recurring component failures. Text mining added value by automating issue
tagging. The findings can directly support warranty cost control and proactive maintenance
decisions.
